A||||14||TRiAD||OK, what to do about messed up front ends...||||1||
Z||000000||TRiAD||11-26-2001||09:48 PM||||Wow, 1000 posts!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>...Like it says, the front is messed up...<BR>I haven't felt any problems with the suspension, but I've only driven it 300 miles, and it's not on the road right now...<P>BUT, there's some interesting paint differences, the "crush" material behind the fascia isn't mounted (it's just sitting in there bouncing around), the turn signals are too high in their holes, and there's significant hood gap. <P>Here's the hood gap...<P> <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0OgBvYQkT7xAQ10rB4cfbuMjoBm6ppS4bvQd!L6ZI2BJVGynOTJOHPO!pKA7be4zcY2TSSZffl9K6B!2t9Vas9X35!8YgR24Q/hood-gap-1.JPG"> <P>And here's it's placement with the rear of the fender, the door, etc..<P> <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0OgAAAAoTGhEQ10rB4cfbuIGckr3!ZzfoUrN1jAgM1CdyOaYqhyoLmUM8sxqTiFmuepTslQI1k!rg94bfQtiG0sB2nnDOPUaA/hood-gap-2.JPG"> <P>So, I'm guessing it's been "bumped" in front at some point, whatchu think? Anything I can do to move the hood back where it goes?<P>Oh, the center of the hood has a real nice gouge in it, right on the point, as well, apparently from contact with the fascia bolt below it when it's open. <P>Ideas? Thanks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0RAAAAN8VWdTblRDE93zkbJ*AbWuOFSwE0rtNS2h23KCe3aGgj1O!JDqud!IoFt1NPvdq6rh9ef*226xJh!nSf*vv2Id5gL8WoDCkDX*tCeM/1985-Signature-2.gif">    <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B> ~ Photoshop rendition of planned modifications</I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||11-26-2001||10:14 PM||||Oh man, I could cry!  What an ordeal you're going through with this car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Consider it a wholeharted and total innitiation into Fiero ownsership, with intrest from all that time spent here withought owning one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (well, either that, or throwing the car off a cliff, trust me, the 'think of it as initiation' way is much more fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>The hood's hinges tend to bend fairly readially in a front-end collision.<P>Energy absorber: bolt it back up if you can, if not, there are lots of used parts dudes on the list whom I am sure can set you up! (you need it, it could save your life, or at least your frame, in a wreck.)<P>It *looks* like your hood has been pushed back (and the rest undamanged/replaced) but it could be the other way around (ie, everything BUT the hood is in the right place...)<P>Can't tell from the pics.<P>Where is this 'honest guy' who sold you this thing?  I feel the need to honestly whip his arse  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||11-26-2001||10:56 PM||||I'd look for hints of spaceframe repair around the headlight buckets.  The hood alignment can be fixed, the hinges have slots.  Kind of a pain, but can be adjusted.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jefrysuko||11-26-2001||11:06 PM||||How is it that you already have 1000 posts?  I remember when I was posting pictures for you before you figured out PIP.  I guess I need to quit being a wall flower.<P>As for the car I have some experience with this.  Both of the 88 GT's I have owned have been in front end dingers.  The first one had a botched up repair job.  The hood on it sat back about twice as far as yours.  The first thing I would recommend for you to do is pull the front nose.  Concentrate on getting the mounting points for the nose back in place.  The most important thing is the top of the nose where it meets the hood.  This is real easy to bend back and gives the nose it's shape.  After you have that taken care of check out the hood hinges.  Must likely they are bent and just need to be straightened to put the hood back where it belongs.  Check out the fenders they can sometimes get pushed back and start to interfere with the doors which looks like a possibility with yours.  Loosen the bolts and see if they will slide forward.  Also pay attention to the molding line between the fender and the lower of the nose and make sure they are lining up right.  Once you have the nose off fixing the mounts for the crush material and the lights isn't a big deal.  If you still have problems or think that the suspension is out of line go and see Bob.  I took mine to him and he had some interesting ways to determine what was bent and what wasn't and didn't charge me a thing to look over it for about an Hour.  As far as the suspension is concerned taking it in for an alignment would be the best way to determine if it is still true.  I would imagine that you should be alright.  Mine looked worse than yours and it passed Bob's tests and the alignment shop.<P>And by the way if you ever do go down to see Bob let me know.  I live a half hour south and would come up to chat.  Also I do go to Peoria once in awhile to see my sister so I will let you know the next time I am heading up and I might be able to help you out a little.<P>Good Luck,||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||11-26-2001||11:22 PM||||Here's a guess or two:<P>The car has been thumped in the front or/and it has been converted from coupe to GT.<P>Either could explain why the "waffle" energy absorber is loose -- the GT waffle shape is different from the coupe, and would have been swapped with the GT nose.  The waffle should be attached by blue plastic push rivets.  I recently saw some in a parts store.<P>Body conversion could account for mal-positioned turn signals, as the location is slightly different among nose styles.<P>At best, there isn't much clearance between the hood and the center fascia mounting screw.  I'm guessing that the nose is bent rearward, causing hood-screw interference, and the hood has been adjusted rearward to clear the screw.  Hood hinges are slotted to allow adjustment.<P>Again, this is speculative, but I think the car was originally a coupe rebodied to GT after a front-end collision.  Maybe you can tell by VIN or RPO codes.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Master_Sushi||11-26-2001||11:23 PM||||I know what to do! Go slap the person for selling you the car. Or yourself for buying it. lol   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>sorry I have nothing helpfull to say, I was somewhat lied to when I bought my fiero and this stuff makes me boilover........<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 11-26-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000006||TRiAD||11-27-2001||11:51 AM||||(grabbing my socket set and checking the mail for that Chilton manual) Time to tear into this, it's not going anywhere, so why wait?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Jeff, I've been to Bob's once, and bought my steel clutch pedal from him (still trying to finish that job), I don't know when I'll get back out there, it'll be after the clutch issues are taken care of for sure. <P>I'm replacing the clutch pedal, and am going to try to bleed the system next (I am going to need assistance with that one...). <P>Anyway thanks for your offer! Let me know when you're up this way.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>...Consider me fully initiated. LOL!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||11-27-2001||12:59 PM||||Taking the front fascia off isn't a huge task, but one of those annoying tasks, IMO.  small bolts behind the hood up top, bolts holding everything together underneath, and bolts (all 7mm I believe) in front of the wheels.  Of course, taking the signal lights out is a piece of cake.  <P>Once off, just start looking for buckling or anything out of the ordinary.  Looks like you have picture capabilities, so just take pics of anything that looks odd and send them our way.<P>I would offer up asking the previous owner about structural damage, but that probably won't bring much information up to the surface for you.<P>Does this problem coupled with the "not actual mileage" problem go together????  Hmmmm, good question.||||reg||1
